WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:06.874
"Do GLP-1 Drugs Like Ozempic (Semaglutide)
Cause Pancreatitis and Pancreatic Cancer?"

00:00:06.874 --> 00:00:08.775
The most common
adverse side effects

00:00:08.775 --> 00:00:10.410
of Ozempic-type
weight-loss drugs

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:12.779
are gastrointestinal issues,

00:00:12.779 --> 00:00:17.050
including nausea, vomiting,
diarrhea, and constipation,

00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:18.785
which I talked about
in a previous video,

00:00:18.785 --> 00:00:20.654
as well as excessive
lean muscle loss

00:00:20.654 --> 00:00:21.688
and gallbladder issues,

00:00:21.688 --> 00:00:23.657
discussed
in a subsequent video,

00:00:23.657 --> 00:00:26.994
but Ozempic may also heighten
the risk of pancreatitis,

00:00:26.994 --> 00:00:29.463
kidney failure,
and thyroid cancer.

00:00:29.463 --> 00:00:32.332
I talked about the thyroid
cancer risk in my last video.

00:00:32.332 --> 00:00:35.469
Many of the kidney issues
may stem from dehydration

00:00:35.469 --> 00:00:37.471
due to excessive vomiting
or diarrhea

00:00:37.471 --> 00:00:38.839
caused by the drugs,

00:00:38.839 --> 00:00:41.275
but what
about the pancreas inflammation?

00:00:41.275 --> 00:00:43.210
Analyzing
the prescription records

00:00:43.210 --> 00:00:45.379
of 16 million Americans,

00:00:45.379 --> 00:00:47.481
compared to taking
an older class

00:00:47.481 --> 00:00:48.682
of weight-loss drugs,

00:00:48.682 --> 00:00:51.251
the use of GLP-1 drugs
like Ozempic

00:00:51.251 --> 00:00:55.188
was associated with nine times
more pancreatitis,

00:00:55.188 --> 00:00:56.657
as well as four times
higher risk

00:00:56.657 --> 00:00:58.458
of bowel obstruction.

00:00:58.458 --> 00:01:01.862
Remember, GLP-1 slows down
your digestive tract.

00:01:01.862 --> 00:01:04.731
That’s part of the reason
people get nauseous and barfy,

00:01:04.731 --> 00:01:07.067
but that’s also part of why
you feel fuller for longer

00:01:07.067 --> 00:01:08.502
and lose weight.

00:01:08.502 --> 00:01:10.938
But sometimes, it can
make your gut so sluggish,

00:01:10.938 --> 00:01:13.006
it stops working,
and you get a blockage

00:01:13.006 --> 00:01:15.375
that could become
a surgical emergency.

00:01:15.375 --> 00:01:17.577
It’s super rare, though.

00:01:17.577 --> 00:01:22.449
The number needed to harm
after one year of use is 1,223,

00:01:22.449 --> 00:01:24.151
meaning that
more than 1,000 people

00:01:24.151 --> 00:01:25.786
would have to take the drug
for a year

00:01:25.786 --> 00:01:28.922
to cause a single extra case
of bowel obstruction.

00:01:28.922 --> 00:01:30.557
So, even after 10 years
of treatment,

00:01:30.557 --> 00:01:34.962
your cumulative risk
would be less than one percent.

00:01:34.962 --> 00:01:38.899
But what about this 800 percent
increased risk of pancreatitis?

00:01:38.899 --> 00:01:42.469
Digestive hormones like GLP-1
act on multiple targets

00:01:42.469 --> 00:01:44.871
within the body,
with multiple effects,

00:01:44.871 --> 00:01:48.508
perhaps more "magic shotgun"
than "magic bullet,"

00:01:48.508 --> 00:01:51.678
but maybe not so magical
for the pancreas.

00:01:51.678 --> 00:01:54.381
GLP-1 receptors are
expressed abundantly

00:01:54.381 --> 00:01:55.649
throughout the pancreas,

00:01:55.649 --> 00:01:58.085
and in response
to GLP-1 therapy,

00:01:58.085 --> 00:02:00.053
the cells of the pancreas
proliferate,

00:02:00.053 --> 00:02:01.188
swelling the organ

00:02:01.188 --> 00:02:03.757
and potentially
squeezing off ducts,

00:02:03.757 --> 00:02:06.893
causing inflammation,
at least in rats,

00:02:06.893 --> 00:02:09.162
but that led to human studies
like this,

00:02:09.162 --> 00:02:10.630
suggesting that GLP-1 drugs

00:02:10.630 --> 00:02:12.432
may increase the odds
of pancreatitis

00:02:12.432 --> 00:02:14.201
as much as six-fold.

00:02:14.201 --> 00:02:16.336
This led some investigators
to conclude

00:02:16.336 --> 00:02:19.106
that the balance of evidence
suggests an association

00:02:19.106 --> 00:02:21.041
between the use
of GLP-1 based therapies

00:02:21.041 --> 00:02:23.744
and acute pancreatitis.

00:02:23.744 --> 00:02:26.179
The major concern
is not pancreatitis, though.

00:02:26.179 --> 00:02:28.782
Acute pancreatitis is
unpleasant enough,

00:02:28.782 --> 00:02:32.853
but the major concern is
subclinical, asymptomatic

00:02:32.853 --> 00:02:34.654
inflammation of the pancreas,

00:02:34.654 --> 00:02:36.723
because inflammation
of the pancreas

00:02:36.723 --> 00:02:39.659
is well known to predispose
to pancreatic cancer,

00:02:39.659 --> 00:02:42.295
which is one of the deadliest
forms of cancer.

00:02:42.295 --> 00:02:46.033
And some of the rats did show
potentially premalignant changes

00:02:46.033 --> 00:02:48.101
in their pancreases
on these drugs.

00:02:48.101 --> 00:02:50.971
The concern is that
the pro-proliferative actions

00:02:50.971 --> 00:02:53.874
of GLP-1 could take
premalignant lesions

00:02:53.874 --> 00:02:57.644
and accelerate their progression
towards cancer.

00:02:57.644 --> 00:03:00.280
Remember how these drugs
were modeled off a compound

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:03.350
found in Gila monster saliva?

00:03:03.350 --> 00:03:07.087
Few have paused to wonder why
they would produce it.

00:03:07.087 --> 00:03:08.855
The answer is that
the Gila monster

00:03:08.855 --> 00:03:11.358
is a desert lizard that goes
for weeks or even months

00:03:11.358 --> 00:03:13.660
between meals
and conserves energy

00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:15.929
during those intervals
by shrinking

00:03:15.929 --> 00:03:18.765
its digestive tract,
including its pancreas.

00:03:18.765 --> 00:03:20.600
So, when it finally does eat,
it needs a way

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:24.037
to rapidly proliferate
its tissues.

00:03:24.037 --> 00:03:26.373
After reviewing thousands
of pages of documents

00:03:26.373 --> 00:03:28.975
obtained through
freedom of information requests,

00:03:28.975 --> 00:03:30.911
a British Medical Journal
investigation

00:03:30.911 --> 00:03:34.514
unearthed unpublished data
from animal and human studies

00:03:34.514 --> 00:03:37.484
that point to pathological
changes in the pancreas,

00:03:37.484 --> 00:03:39.119
including attempts
by drug companies

00:03:39.119 --> 00:03:41.321
to suppress scientific debate

00:03:41.321 --> 00:03:43.390
by withholding
important safety data

00:03:43.390 --> 00:03:45.225
from the public.

00:03:45.225 --> 00:03:47.627
Why have the companies been
so slow to respond

00:03:47.627 --> 00:03:48.895
to this threat?

00:03:48.895 --> 00:03:51.631
Because of
the "three monkey paradigm,"

00:03:51.631 --> 00:03:54.267
which operates as follows.

00:03:54.267 --> 00:03:56.136
Companies are
legally responsible

00:03:56.136 --> 00:03:58.705
for monitoring the safety
of their own products,

00:03:58.705 --> 00:04:00.807
but obviously can’t be
held responsible

00:04:00.807 --> 00:04:04.311
for tackling a safety concern
that does not exist.

00:04:04.311 --> 00:04:07.214
A concern that can be
plausibly doubted or denied

00:04:07.214 --> 00:04:09.816
carries no legal liability,

00:04:09.816 --> 00:04:11.918
whereas one that gives rise
to serious consideration

00:04:11.918 --> 00:04:14.421
leaves the door wide open
to lawsuits.

00:04:14.421 --> 00:04:17.424
Thus, inviting companies
to monitor the safety

00:04:17.424 --> 00:04:19.693
of their own products
provides them

00:04:19.693 --> 00:04:21.728
with the strongest
possible incentive

00:04:21.728 --> 00:04:23.430
for failing to do so,

00:04:23.430 --> 00:04:26.533
an instance of the law
of unintended consequences.

00:04:26.533 --> 00:04:28.401
The three monkeys,
who neither hear

00:04:28.401 --> 00:04:29.970
nor see nor speak,

00:04:29.970 --> 00:04:32.372
have been allowed to flourish
at the heart of our system

00:04:32.372 --> 00:04:34.541
for protecting the public.

00:04:34.541 --> 00:04:36.376
"After careful reflection,"

00:04:36.376 --> 00:04:38.645
wrote a professor of medicine
at the Mayo Clinic,

00:04:38.645 --> 00:04:41.848
"most patients and clinicians
may opt to avoid

00:04:41.848 --> 00:04:44.184
GLP-1 based drugs
or at least avoid using them

00:04:44.184 --> 00:04:46.419
for a prolonged
period of time."

00:04:46.419 --> 00:04:50.090
And this was all before
this pivotal study came out,

00:04:50.090 --> 00:04:53.693
where researchers obtained
transplant-quality pancreases

00:04:53.693 --> 00:04:55.328
from, like, accident victims

00:04:55.328 --> 00:04:58.198
who had been taking GLP-1 drugs
for at least a year

00:04:58.198 --> 00:04:59.499
before they died,

00:04:59.499 --> 00:05:03.770
and every single pancreas
showed abnormalities,

00:05:03.770 --> 00:05:08.275
marked enlargement, dysplasia,
and even a few little tumors.

00:05:08.275 --> 00:05:11.678
This was all discovered
more than a decade ago, though.

00:05:11.678 --> 00:05:12.946
Where are we now?

00:05:12.946 --> 00:05:16.583
It remains unclear
whether the use of GLP-1 drugs

00:05:16.583 --> 00:05:19.886
is linked to an increased risk
of pancreatic cancer.

00:05:19.886 --> 00:05:21.922
But trials
would have to be enormous

00:05:21.922 --> 00:05:23.523
to exclude an increased risk,

00:05:23.523 --> 00:05:25.892
and unless the rules
change radically

00:05:25.892 --> 00:05:27.727
by the time
they are to be reported,

00:05:27.727 --> 00:05:29.696
most of the data
will remain hidden

00:05:29.696 --> 00:05:31.398
from independent scrutiny.

00:05:31.398 --> 00:05:34.434
And as one Johns Hopkins
drug safety researcher asked,

00:05:34.434 --> 00:05:37.003
"The fundamental question is
who bears the burden

00:05:37.003 --> 00:05:38.038
of the passage of time

00:05:38.038 --> 00:05:41.308
while these debates
are settled?"

00:05:41.308 --> 00:05:43.376
Thousands
of pancreatic cancer cases

00:05:43.376 --> 00:05:47.347
have been reportedly tied
to GLP-1 drugs, like Ozempic,

00:05:47.347 --> 00:05:49.382
based on the U.S. Food
and Drug Administration’s

00:05:49.382 --> 00:05:52.085
Adverse Events Reporting System,

00:05:52.085 --> 00:05:55.722
but adverse event databases
that rely on voluntary reporting

00:05:55.722 --> 00:05:58.959
are limited by the potential
for reporting bias.

00:05:58.959 --> 00:06:00.894
That’s like the Centers
for Disease Control’s

00:06:00.894 --> 00:06:02.262
VAERS database,

00:06:02.262 --> 00:06:05.232
the Vaccine Adverse Events
Reporting System.

00:06:05.232 --> 00:06:06.633
During COVID,

00:06:06.633 --> 00:06:10.036
the more states were inclined
to vote Republican,

00:06:10.036 --> 00:06:12.839
the more likely their vaccine
recipients or clinicians

00:06:12.839 --> 00:06:15.675
were to report
COVID vaccine side effects;

00:06:15.675 --> 00:06:18.545
so, maybe there was some kind
of similar reporting bias

00:06:18.545 --> 00:06:20.747
among those who submit
their pancreatic cancer

00:06:20.747 --> 00:06:22.282
to the FDA?

00:06:22.282 --> 00:06:24.684
Either way, if there is
even a remote chance

00:06:24.684 --> 00:06:26.953
that certain drugs
can have an impact

00:06:26.953 --> 00:06:28.521
on cancer development
and progression,

00:06:28.521 --> 00:06:32.225
then such risks
should not be taken lightly.

00:06:33.660 --> 00:06:36.129
But if you really don’t want
to get cancer,

00:06:36.129 --> 00:06:39.132
then you really don’t want
to be overweight.

00:06:39.132 --> 00:06:40.800
Based on a study
following millions of people,

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:42.736
the longer you’re overweight
or obese,

00:06:42.736 --> 00:06:44.471
the higher your apparent risk

00:06:44.471 --> 00:06:47.140
of 18 different kinds of cancer.

00:06:47.140 --> 00:06:49.776
So, even if you cared
only about cancer,

00:06:49.776 --> 00:06:53.613
might these drugs actually lower
your overall cancer risk

00:06:53.613 --> 00:06:55.982
if they caused
enough weight loss?

00:06:55.982 --> 00:06:59.185
A meta-analysis
of bariatric surgery studies

00:06:59.185 --> 00:07:00.987
found that those
going under the knife

00:07:00.987 --> 00:07:03.056
tend to lose so much weight

00:07:03.056 --> 00:07:04.958
that they have
a significantly lower risk

00:07:04.958 --> 00:07:05.992
of getting cancer

00:07:05.992 --> 00:07:07.861
and appear to cut
their risk of dying from cancer

00:07:07.861 --> 00:07:09.696
nearly in half.

00:07:09.696 --> 00:07:12.465
This modeling study suggests
that widespread usage

00:07:12.465 --> 00:07:15.602
of these new-generation
weight-loss drugs, like Ozempic,

00:07:15.602 --> 00:07:19.139
could prevent more than,
like, 1 out of 100 cancers

00:07:19.139 --> 00:07:20.874
over the next 25 years.

00:07:20.874 --> 00:07:22.442
And that’s just cancer.

00:07:22.442 --> 00:07:25.011
Wouldn’t just
the cardiovascular benefits

00:07:25.011 --> 00:07:28.715
of losing a lot of weight alone
outweigh any potential risks?

00:07:28.715 --> 00:07:31.051
That’s the subject
of my next video:

00:07:31.051 --> 00:07:33.853
Do the benefits
outweigh the risks?

